**Read:** [Inorganic Chemical Technology — Nanda Kishore, NPTEL](../sources/Inorganic-Chemical-Technology-Kishore.lingua#r_77Z8T5RFYFNNXZ7XKZFKPRKDKR), the entire urea/nitrogen-fertiliser manufacturing treatment; [Large Volume Inorganic Chemicals — Ammonia, Acids and Fertilisers, BREF LVIC-AAF](../sources/Ammonia-Acids-Fertilisers-BREF.lingua#r_45NWBJYAMXXSRV071A57EM02GB), the complete urea and connected ammonia/utility/product chapters; [Industrial Chemical Technology II — Nduka, NOUN](../sources/Industrial-Chemical-Technology-II-NOUN.lingua#r_7RXE2GF1DW7QH9GHTCGT0P09SR), the full feedstock, route and technical-account context.

The net relation 2NH₃+CO₂→CO(NH₂)₂+H₂O links urea to ammonia and carbon dioxide, but the actual process includes intermediate chemistry, equilibrium and recovery of unconverted material. Distinguish a balanced overall equation from the reactions and phases through which a production route proceeds.

Reaction/separation, returned ammonia/carbon dioxide, water management, heat and equipment materials form a coupled system. A high reactor conversion does not determine the final nutrient product: concentration, finishing, particle properties, moisture and consequential byproducts can affect storage, handling and use.

Preserve the nitrogen and carbon accounts through co-products, emissions, wastewater and changing inventories. Compare product grades and downstream functions on an actual nutrient and quality basis. A circulating material returned to synthesis is not new feed or an additional saleable product.

**Work:** reconstruct a complete urea route using the full primary technical chapter, independently closing nitrogen, carbon and water/energy accounts. Compare two documented recovery/finishing arrangements at equal accepted product output. Identify the reaction, phase, corrosion, physical-form and measurement evidence needed to distinguish their consequential differences.

